  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "The graph of the linear function \\(y=2x+4\\) intersects the x-axis at \\((-2,0)\\), and \\(y\\) increases as \\(x\\) increases. The solution set of the inequality \\(2x+4>0\\) is ( ).",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x<-2\\)",
    "B": "\\(x>-2\\)",
    "C": "\\(x>2\\)"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "\\(y>0\\) corresponds to the graph being above the x-axis. Since \\(y\\) increases as \\(x\\) increases, this occurs to the right of the intersection, that is, \\(x>-2\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Solve the inequality algebraically\n\nSubtracting \\(4\\) from both sides isolates the term containing \\(x\\): \\(2x>-4\\). Dividing by the positive number \\(2\\) keeps the inequality direction unchanged. Since the original inequality is strict, the solution set is \\(x>-2\\), choice B.",
